module j3.data.set
    Hash set `Set<T>`.
    `Set<string>.new(&arena, 16)` is a `j3.data.map.Map<T, u8>` underneath, so the same rules hold: `T` needs
    `Hash` and `Eq`, memory comes from the arena, `clear` keeps the slots, order is unspecified
    (`sorted(&arena)` for a deterministic one, `T: Ord`). Not shared across threads.

struct Set<T: Hash + Eq>
    m: Map<T, u8>
    fn Set<T: Hash + Eq>.new(arena: &Arena, cap: int): Set<T>
        Empty set with room for about `cap` items before the first growth.
    fn Set<T: Hash + Eq>.add(this: &Set<T>, x: T): bool
        Add `x`; true when it was not there yet.
    fn Set<T: Hash + Eq>.has(this: &Set<T>, x: T): bool
        True when `x` is in the set.
    fn Set<T: Hash + Eq>.remove(this: &Set<T>, x: T): bool
        Remove `x`; false when it was not there.
    fn Set<T: Hash + Eq>.len(this: &Set<T>): int
        Number of items.
    fn Set<T: Hash + Eq>.clear(this: &Set<T>)
        Drop every item, keeping the slots.
    fn Set<T: Hash + Eq>.each(this: &Set<T>, f: closure(T))
        Call `f(x)` for every item (order unspecified); `f` must not add or remove.
    fn Set<T: Hash + Eq>.sorted(this: &Set<T>, arena: &Arena): []T
        Every item, sorted ascending (`T: Ord`), in a slice from `arena`.

